found.summary:
Upstream bpftime source, read-only; ran git fetch official on 2026-11-13. official/master moved from 1e39125a to 42cf71c4.

(a) official/master since 39cc1fce has 11 commits (fa842ad7 .. 42cf71c4, 2026-11-11..12). None touch the GPU map types 1502/1512/1527/1503/1513, thread counts, the GPU ringbuf, warp-leader output, make_helper_call or the CUDA watcher.
- The only GPU-side commit is 466e91b0 (#740): it makes the mem_trace example counter atomic, because lockstep lanes lost 9 of 10 non-atomic updates.
- 8e257dd2 (#735) tests the CPU userspace ringbuf, not 1527.

On master today, direction 1 is covered only by these merged pieces:
- an unvalidated BPFTIME_MAP_GPU_THREAD_COUNT (default 1024);
- a per-access device printf for 1502/1512, which then writes past the map anyway;
- a silent `return 1` for ringbuf threads beyond the count;
- halving of the ringbuf thread count when allocation fails (bad_alloc);
- hardware-position helpers 508-511 plus one hand-written SM/warp histogram example;
- documentation telling users to set the env var by hand.

(b) There are 7 official/revision/* branches, all by LinuxDev9002, dated 2026-09-03..09-24. None is merged and none has a PR:
- `git cherry` finds no equivalents;
- master has 0 hits for their symbols;
- no PR exists with these heads.

Five of them (launchlate-finish, table1-575, table1-host-plt-fix, sass-*, automatic-warp-execution) share one commit chain on merge-base 1dd02f76 (2026-08-09). That chain contains the cheap fixes:
- validation of the thread-count env (only 1502 and 1527, cap 1048576, -EINVAL);
- per-reason drop counters and a stats API for 1527 only;
- the per-thread array still writes out of bounds;
- no warning at launch on any ref.

automatic-warp-execution adds:
- an opt-in warp-only output flag (bit 63) for perf_event_output: the first active lane writes, the other lanes' records are dropped, nothing is aggregated, it still uses per-thread slots, and no tool uses it;
- ringbuf transport levels 1-3: still per-thread and per-event, and memory is unchanged;
- 'automatic warp execution'.

Automatic warp execution is opt-in (BPFTIME_GPU_AUTO_WARP_EXECUTION and a build with the verifier). It is a yes/no decision per program, not per call site:
- It admits a program only if the full SIMT verifier accepts it and every side effect is idempotent across the warp, i.e. stores to shared GPU maps (1501/1503/1504/1513) with warp-uniform values.
- It rejects any atomic, perf_event_output, trace_printk, puts and cuda_exit, and any write to per-thread maps 1502/1512.
- It then rewrites each kretprobe, kernel-entry or stub hook site so that only the lowest lane among those active at the site with the site predicate true (activemask plus vote.ballot.sync) calls the handler.
- Results are exact by construction. There is no aggregation, deferral, or commutativity or result-usage analysis.
- It excludes exactly the programs that use direction 1's maps.
- Its eligibility test fixtures were never compiled or run.
- It depends on the GPU SIMT verifier, which is only in open PR #624.

So for direction 1:
- size_at_launch, a shared or per-SM warp buffer, and hardware-slot map types are absent from both master and every branch;
- cheap fixes exist only partially and only on an unmerged branch.

For direction 2, the only related piece is the narrow exact deduplication of idempotent programs (leader-only execution). Because it runs the whole handler once per warp, it also removes duplicate host calls such as lookups and 1501 updates.

gh worked. Since 2026-09 there are no PRs or issues about thread_count, max_thread_count, warp leaders or automatic warp. The relevant items found are #739/#740, #752 and #697/#698/#706/#735 (CPU ringbuf), plus the older #530 (2025-12).

findings.claim:
Automatic warp execution: the eligibility rules (bpftime-verifier/src/gpu/warp_execution_eligibility.cpp at tip).

Prerequisite (:233): verify_gpu_program must pass in full, i.e. PREVAIL plus uniformity analysis plus SIMT safety. The SIMT check itself already requires:
- warp-uniform conditional-branch predicates (simt_safety_check.cpp:213);
- warp-uniform map keys (:264);
- uniform values in shared-map updates;
- a closed helper table (gpu_platform.cpp:108-172): helpers 1, 2, 3, 6, 14, 25 and 501-511, with 501 puts and 506 membar prohibited.

Then any reachable instruction rejects the whole program if it is:
- an atomic: 'atomic side effects are lane-multiplicative' (:133). No commutativity reasoning; 32 atomic adds are not turned into one add of 32.
- a plain store through an untracked pointer (:148), or into a map value that may be a per-thread slot (:154);
- a call to helper 25 perf_event_output, 6 trace_printk, 501 puts or 507 cuda_exit (:164-171);
- a map update/delete on a per-thread map 1502/1512 (:192), on a map that is not a shared GPU map (1501/1503/1504/1513) (:196), or with update value bytes not proven warp-uniform (:206).

Allowed: lookups on any map, helper 14, and timers/indices.

Consequences:
- Programs using direction 1's maps (1502/1512 writes, 1527 output) are never eligible.
- No result-usage analysis exists beyond uniformity.
- For direction 2, eligible programs do lose their duplicate host round trips, such as lookups on CPU maps or 1501 (which have no device fast path), helper 14, and uniform 1501 updates. The authors never state this, and it covers the 'eliminate idempotent duplicates' case only.

not_found:
Automatic warp execution features absent even on the branch:
- a per-call-site decision;
- aggregation of commutative updates (atomics are rejected outright);
- deferring or buffering helper effects and applying them later with exact final state;
- any analysis of result usage or commutativity;
- support for programs using perf_event_output or per-thread maps.

