added.claim:
Code-reading inference, not executed: automatic warp execution would admit a non-atomic read-modify-write counter (e.g. `*val = *val + 1` on 1503/1501 or on a CPU-map value with a constant key), because:
- a load from a shared-map lookup is UNIFORM;
- a plain store of a uniform value into a shared-map value passes both the SIMT check and the eligibility rules;
- only atomics are rejected.

Leader-only execution then reproduces the per-warp collapsed count that #739 reported as a bug (fixed in the example by #740 with an atomic, which AWE would reject). The analysis has no notion of non-idempotent plain read-modify-write, and no fixture covers this case.

official/review/pr-253-fix 7356191c (2026-09-05, LinuxDev9002; no PR) contains an earlier squash-style copy of the tool changes:
- threadhist reads BPFTIME_MAP_GPU_THREAD_COUNT (default 1024) and refuses values above 1024*1024 ('exceeds tool capacity');
- kernelretsnoop records block and thread indices;
- BPFTIME_CUDA_TARGETED_LATE_BOOTSTRAP is added.
The sizing checks are tool-side only and add no runtime check.

Negative check across all 63 official/* refs: no ref sizes or grows GPU map storage from the grid. git grep for grid_dim/gridDim/grid_size in runtime/src/bpf_map and runtime/src/handler finds nothing on any ref.

